  <h2>The LinkedIn template problem</h2>
  <p>People scroll past posts that sound alike. The giveaways are familiar: "game-changing", "unlock the full potential", "stay ahead of the curve". They add length and say nothing a reader can picture. Short posts leave nowhere to hide, so a single stock phrase can sink the whole thing.</p>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <blockquote><p><strong>Before:</strong> Our innovative platform has been a game changer. We are able to unlock the full potential of our data and make informed decisions.</p></blockquote>
  <blockquote><p><strong>After:</strong> Our creative platform has been a major shift. We can get the most from our data and make better decisions.</p></blockquote>
  <p>That is cleaner, but it is still vague. A rules engine stops there. The post gets good when you replace "a major shift" with what actually changed: a number, a date or a story.</p>

  <h2>Settings for short posts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Use <strong>Natural</strong> tone. LinkedIn reads better with contractions.</li>
    <li>Use <strong>Strong</strong> strength, since adjective swaps matter most here, then read every line in the log. With so few words, one odd swap stands out.</li>
    <li>Short lines without end punctuation are treated like headings and stay unchanged, so a one-line hook is safe.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to add yourself</h2>
  <p>Start with something only you could say. A specific result, an opinion someone might disagree with, or a mistake you made will do more than any edit. Then cut the closing question if it exists only to farm comments. Our guide on <a href="/guides/add-your-own-voice">adding your own voice</a> has exercises for this.</p>
  <h2>Posts, comments and your profile</h2>
  <p>The same cleanup helps anywhere on LinkedIn where AI drafts show up:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Posts.</strong> Buzz phrases stand out most here, because there are so few words around them.</li>
    <li><strong>The About section.</strong> AI-written summaries lean on "results-driven professional with a proven track record". Run the draft, then replace each remaining claim with one thing you actually did.</li>
    <li><strong>Comments and messages.</strong> A stiff, over-polished comment reads as automated. Contractions and plain words fix most of that.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Stay inside the length limit</h2>
  <p>A LinkedIn post can run to about 3,000 characters, and only the first few lines show before the "see more" link. Removing filler helps with both: the post gets shorter, and your real opening line moves up. Use the <a href="/word-counter">word counter</a> to check the character count before you post.</p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <h3>Will it remove my hashtags or mentions?</h3>
  <p>No. Hashtags, @mentions and links are locked and stay exactly as written.</p>
  <h3>Does it keep emojis and line breaks?</h3>
  <p>Yes. The editor only changes the words its rules match, so emojis, blank lines and short one-line hooks are left alone.</p>
  <h3>Can it make my post go viral?</h3>
  <p>No tool can promise that. It can stop a post from sounding like a template, which gives your own idea a fairer chance.</p>
